    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>No pill replaces sunscreen, and no oral supplement should be treated as one.</strong> Oral Polypodium leucotomos raised the UV dose skin tolerates before reddening by about 8% in the best documented 12-week trial, which is a protection ratio near 1.1 against 30 for an SPF 30 cream applied properly.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>It does not filter UV:</strong> the extract is an antioxidant taken by mouth, so nothing sits on the skin to absorb or scatter ultraviolet light.</li>
        <li><strong>The measured effect is small:</strong> 8.1% higher minimal erythema dose at day 84 in 30 subjects, and 23.8% in an 8-week trial of a combination product that also contained red orange extract and four vitamins.</li>
        <li><strong>A regulator has said so plainly:</strong> the US FDA wrote to four companies in May 2018 over supplements marketed as sunscreen.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limit on our own shot:</strong> Collabloom carries 240mg of Polypodium leucotomos once daily, which is the lowest end of the studied range and a quarter of the daily amount used in the larger melasma trial.</li>
      </ul>
