      <h2 id="does-it-work">Does an oral sunscreen actually work?</h2>
      <p>It does something measurable, and that something is much smaller than the name suggests. The phrase "oral sunscreen" is doing most of the work in the marketing, because a sunscreen is a regulated product with a tested number on the front of the bottle, and a supplement is neither of those things.</p>
      <p>The ingredient under nearly all of these products is an extract of <em>Polypodium leucotomos</em>, a fern from Central and South America, usually sold as the branded extract Fernblock. It has a genuine research record. A 2025 systematic review in the <em>Journal of Drugs in Dermatology</em> screened 152 articles and included 21, among them 11 randomised controlled trials, and concluded that the extract shows therapeutic potential with an encouraging safety profile. That is a real body of work, and it is worth saying so before taking it apart.</p>
      <p>What it is not is a body of work that supports replacing sunscreen. An earlier systematic review focused specifically on photoprotection included 18 studies in which sample sizes ran from 5 to 61 people. Most reported a benefit, measured as an increase in the minimal erythema dose, and none reported serious adverse effects. Small studies, consistent direction, modest size of effect: that is the fair summary, and it is the summary the category tends to leave out.</p>

