      <h2 id="how-it-works">How is a sunscreen pill supposed to work?</h2>
      <p>By changing how skin responds to UV that has already reached it, not by stopping the UV arriving. This single distinction answers most of the question.</p>
      <p>A topical sunscreen is a physical barrier. Mineral and chemical filters sit in the top layer of skin and absorb or scatter ultraviolet photons before they reach living cells. That is why application technique matters so much, why the quantity you use changes the protection you get, and why reapplication exists at all.</p>
      <p>An oral extract cannot do any of that. It is absorbed, distributed in the blood, and acts inside the skin as an antioxidant: mopping up reactive oxygen species generated when UV hits tissue, and limiting some of the downstream inflammatory and DNA damage. Indian dermatologists quoted on the subject make the same point, that the extract does not block UV radiation and so may improve the skin's resistance to damage without filtering anything.</p>
      <p>That mechanism has a ceiling built into it. UV still reaches the dermis in full. The supplement is working on the consequences, after the fact, which is a fundamentally weaker position than preventing the exposure. It is also why the honest framing in every clinical paper is adjunct, not alternative. Ultraviolet exposure is the single largest external driver of collagen breakdown in skin, which we covered in <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">how to boost collagen naturally</a>, and the intervention with the evidence there is still the cream you reapply.</p>

