      <h2 id="numbers">How much protection does Polypodium leucotomos add?</h2>
      <p>Enough to measure in a laboratory, not enough to plan a day outdoors around. The outcome almost every trial uses is the minimal erythema dose, or MED: the smallest amount of UV energy that produces visible redness on your skin. If a supplement raises your MED, it takes slightly more sun to burn you.</p>
      <p>Three results give the honest range. A 12-week study in <em>Dermatology and Therapy</em> in 2019 followed 30 subjects and found MED rose significantly against baseline throughout, reaching an increase of 8.1% at day 84. A 2025 randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in 54 fair-skinned adults with Fitzpatrick phototypes I to III reported a larger effect after eight weeks, with MED up about 23.8% and the intensity of UVB-induced redness down about 46.2%. And a 2025 study of a Polypodium gummy taken at 240mg twice daily for five days found MED improvement in 64% of 25 completers by visual grading and 80% by colorimetry.</p>
      <p>Each of those carries a caveat that matters. The 23.8% figure is the strongest in the set and it is not a Polypodium result: the product tested also contained red orange extract and vitamins A, C, D and E, so the contribution of the fern cannot be separated out. The gummy study had no placebo arm, which for a visually graded skin endpoint is a serious limitation. The 8.1% trial is the cleanest look at a single supplement over a meaningful period, and it is also the smallest number.</p>
      <p>Now the arithmetic that the marketing never prints. Sun protection factor is defined as a ratio: the MED of protected skin divided by the MED of unprotected skin. An 8.1% rise in MED is a ratio of about 1.08. The 23.8% rise, from a combination product, is about 1.24. Dermatologists recommend a broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher, which is a ratio of 30 or more. You are comparing a tenth of a factor against thirty factors.</p>
      <p>Two qualifications, in fairness. No regulator assigns an SPF to an oral product, and MED in these trials is not measured the way SPF testing is standardised, so the comparison is illustrative rather than official. And a widely repeated claim that the extract is equivalent to an extra SPF 3 or 4 traces back to blog posts with no study attached to it. We could not find a primary source for that figure, so we are not citing it.</p>

