      <h2 id="melasma">Does it help melasma and pigmentation?</h2>
      <p>This is where the evidence is most interesting and still comes with the same answer: studied alongside proper treatment, never instead of it. Both of the pigmentation trials worth knowing about gave every participant sunscreen, and one gave everyone a prescription cream as well.</p>
      <p>A double-blind randomised study tabulated in a 2014 review of pigmentary disorders compared the oral extract plus sunscreen against placebo plus sunscreen in two small groups of 16 and 17 people over 12 weeks. The melanin index improved 28.8% with the extract against 13.8% with placebo, while the melasma area and severity index improved in both groups and quality-of-life scores moved in neither. The published dose is reported inconsistently between that review and the trial registry for a melasma study of the same design, so we would not quote a figure for it.</p>
      <p>The more relevant trial for Indian readers is Goh and colleagues in the <em>Journal of Clinical and Aesthetic Dermatology</em> in 2018. Forty adults in Singapore with facial melasma, Fitzpatrick skin types III and IV, were randomised to the oral extract or placebo for 12 weeks. Everybody was already using 4% hydroquinone and an SPF 50 or higher sunscreen. Scores improved significantly from baseline in both arms, and by the end of the study the difference between the two groups was not significant, although the extract group was ahead at the eight and twelve week visits. The trial was sponsored by the company that manufactures the extract, and its abstract concludes more confidently than the end-of-study comparison supports.</p>
      <p>So: a possible adjunct benefit, in small industry-linked trials, on top of a prescription cream and a high SPF. The same 2014 review notes that no controlled study has assessed the extract for the dark marks left after inflammation, which is the more common pigmentation complaint in Indian clinics. We write about the oral ingredient with the better pigmentation evidence in <a href="/pages/glutathione-for-skin">glutathione for skin</a>, and about what actually works on post-acne marks in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-acne-scars">collagen and acne scars</a>. Nothing here is a treatment for melasma, and melasma genuinely needs a dermatologist rather than a supplement aisle.</p>

      <h2 id="fda">Why did the FDA write to sunscreen pill companies?</h2>
      <p>Because they were selling protection they had not demonstrated. On 22 May 2018 the US Food and Drug Administration sent warning letters to four companies marketing dietary supplements with sun protection claims, among them products named Sunsafe Rx, Solaricare, Sunergetic and a skin brightening formula.</p>
      <p>The agency's objection was specific. The letters quoted marketing language including "enhances photoprotection" and "it is basically an oral sunscreen", and said the products gave consumers a false sense of security because they do not protect against skin cancer, sunburn or early skin ageing in the way the labels claimed. The then commissioner put it in one sentence: there is no pill or capsule that can replace your sunscreen. At least one company disputed the characterisation and said it had never told customers to drop other sun protection.</p>
      <p>That episode is the reason this article exists in the form it does. We sell a shot that contains 240mg of this extract, and the sensible thing to do with a regulator's finding that cuts against your own category is to print it.</p>

      <h2 id="india">Does any of this matter more in India?</h2>
      <p>The exposure case is stronger here and the regulatory position is weaker, which is an awkward combination. India sits at latitudes with high year-round UV, outdoor work is common, and sunscreen use is both lower and more grudging than in the markets where these trials were run.</p>
      <p>Indian dermatologists interviewed about oral sunscreens in the Indian press have been consistent. One told <em>Business Standard</em> in 2026 that they should be used only as an adjunct and never a replacement, and that they cannot match the direct UV-blocking power of a topical product. A dermatologist quoted in <em>Harper's Bazaar India</em> recommends an oral capsule to some patients while another in the same piece insists they are adjuncts rather than replacements. A Hyderabad dermatologist made the point that carries the most weight: there is currently no recognition of oral sunblocks by the Indian drug controlling authority. There is no approved category, so there is no approved claim.</p>
      <p>There is also a specifically Indian version of the risk the FDA described. If a capsule or a shot persuades someone that the 2pm sun is now negotiable, it has made their skin worse, not better, whatever its antioxidant effect. The practical answer is unglamorous: a broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher applied generously, reapplied roughly every two hours when you are out, plus shade, a cap and sleeves. The trials themselves ran on top of exactly that.</p>

      <h2 id="dose">What dose was studied, and what is in Collabloom?</h2>
      <p>Collabloom contains 240mg of Polypodium leucotomos per 30ml shot, taken once a day. That sits at the bottom of the range the research has used, and most of the pigmentation work used considerably more.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>240mg once daily</strong> is what a small 10-subject pilot used over 15 days, and it is what our shot provides.</li>
        <li><strong>240mg twice daily</strong>, so 480mg a day, was used in the dedicated safety and efficacy study in healthy adults over 60 days, and in the 2025 gummy study.</li>
        <li><strong>480mg twice daily</strong>, so 960mg a day, was the dose in the Goh melasma trial. That is four times what a daily Collabloom shot delivers.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>We would rather say that plainly than let the ingredient list imply something the dose does not support. The 240mg in Collabloom is a sensible antioxidant inclusion in a formula built around 8,000mg of marine collagen for skin quality. It is not a sun protection dose, it is not a sunscreen, and nobody should wear less sunscreen because they drank it. The same honesty applies to several of our co-factors, and we set out the general principle in <a href="/pages/how-much-collagen-per-day">how much collagen per day</a>.</p>

