      <h2 id="safety">Is Polypodium leucotomos safe?</h2>
      <p>The record is reassuring and thinner than it looks. A 2015 status report reviewed 19 human studies and 6 laboratory studies and found side effects in 16 of 1,016 patients, about 2%, consisting mostly of mild to moderate stomach upset and itching. It concluded the extract was well tolerated at every dose given.</p>
      <p>The authors of that review added the caveat that matters: no study has ever been published with the safety of the extract as its primary aim, so every safety conclusion rests on adverse events noted in passing during efficacy trials. A placebo-controlled study of 240mg twice daily for 60 days in healthy adults found no changes on any safety assessment, with four treated subjects reporting mild fatigue, bloating or headache that the investigators did not attribute to treatment.</p>
      <p>What is missing is long-term and special-population data. The trials are short, the samples are small, and there is no safety information for pregnancy or breastfeeding, which is why standard references advise avoiding it in both. Our own position on our products in pregnancy is the same, and the broader picture on tolerability is in <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">collagen side effects</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="see-a-doctor">When to see a doctor</h2>
      <p>Sun damage is mostly slow and cosmetic. A few things on skin are not, and no supplement belongs anywhere near them.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A mole or dark patch that changes in size, shape, colour or border, bleeds, itches persistently or looks different from every other mark on you.</li>
        <li>A sore, scaly patch or a lump that does not heal within a few weeks, particularly on the face, ears, scalp, forearms or lower legs.</li>
        <li>A rash, blistering or intense itching that appears reliably after sun exposure, which can indicate a photosensitivity disorder and needs diagnosis rather than an antioxidant.</li>
        <li>Sudden or spreading pigmentation, or melasma that is worsening despite sunscreen, which is a dermatology consultation and often a prescription.</li>
        <li>Any sun sensitivity that began after starting a new medicine. Several common drugs, including some antibiotics, diuretics and retinoids, make skin burn far faster than usual.</li>
      </ul>

