      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does oral sunscreen actually work?</summary>
          <p>It produces a small, measurable change rather than protection you can rely on. The best documented 12-week trial found the UV dose needed to redden skin rose 8.1% in 30 subjects, and an 8-week trial of a combination product reported 23.8%. Expressed the way sun protection factor is defined, those are ratios of about 1.1 and 1.2 against 30 for an SPF 30 cream.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I stop wearing sunscreen if I take an oral sunscreen?</summary>
          <p>No. The extract does not filter ultraviolet light, so nothing is stopping UV from reaching your skin. Every clinical source describes oral photoprotection as an adjunct to topical sunscreen, and the trials that found a benefit gave sunscreen to everyone taking part. The US FDA warned four companies in 2018 precisely because this claim gives people a false sense of security.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is Polypodium leucotomos?</summary>
          <p>It is an extract of a fern native to Central and South America, sold most often as the branded extract Fernblock. Taken by mouth it acts as an antioxidant inside the skin, reducing some of the oxidative and inflammatory damage that follows UV exposure. A 2025 systematic review included 21 studies on it, of which 11 were randomised controlled trials.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does an oral sunscreen help melasma?</summary>
          <p>There is limited evidence that it may add something when used alongside proper treatment. In a 12-week trial of 40 adults with Fitzpatrick skin types III and IV, everyone used 4% hydroquinone and SPF 50 sunscreen, both groups improved, and the between-group difference was not significant at the end of the study. That trial was funded by the extract's manufacturer. Melasma needs a dermatologist, not a supplement.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What are the side effects of Polypodium leucotomos?</summary>
          <p>A 2015 review of 19 human studies found side effects in 16 of 1,016 patients, about 2%, mainly mild stomach upset and itching. No study has been published with safety as its primary aim, the trials are short, and there is no safety data for pregnancy or breastfeeding, so it should be avoided in both.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is oral sunscreen approved in India?</summary>
          <p>There is no approved category for it. A dermatologist quoted in the Indian press made the point that the Indian drug controlling authority does not currently recognise oral sunblocks, which means no product can carry an approved sun protection claim. Indian dermatologists interviewed on the subject describe these supplements consistently as adjuncts and never replacements.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much Polypodium leucotomos is in Collabloom, and is that a sun protection dose?</summary>
          <p>Collabloom contains 240mg per 30ml shot, once daily. That matches the lowest dose used in the research, a 10-subject pilot over 15 days. The safety and efficacy study used 240mg twice daily and the Goh melasma trial used 480mg twice daily, which is four times our daily amount. It is an antioxidant co-factor in the formula, not a sun protection dose.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does taking it prevent tanning or sunburn?</summary>
          <p>Not dependably. Raising the minimal erythema dose by 8% to 24% means it takes slightly more UV to redden your skin, which is not the same as preventing a burn on a long day outdoors. No trial has tested it as a way to avoid tanning, and the sensible reading is that it shifts a threshold slightly rather than removing the risk.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

